WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.360 --> 00:00:05.640
Ever since its discovery in 1985,
the springtime ozone hole

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:08.760
over Antarctica has been
an insistent reminder of

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:12.480
humankind’s ability to cause
environmental harm.

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:14.740
But the wound has begun to heal:

00:00:14.740 --> 00:00:18.020
there is now evidence that
the hole is shrinking.

00:00:18.030 --> 00:00:20.290
The problem began in the 1980s

00:00:20.290 --> 00:00:22.610
with chlorine-containing
chemicals used in

00:00:22.610 --> 00:00:26.360
dry cleaning, refrigerators, and aerosol cans.

00:00:26.360 --> 00:00:29.220
In order to destroy ozone,
the chlorine needs

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:32.280
to be activated with two extra ingredients --

00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:35.340
ultraviolet light and
stratospheric clouds

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:39.620
made of nitric acid particles that
only form in extreme cold.

00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:44.070
That’s why Antarctic ozone
destruction peaks in October --

00:00:44.070 --> 00:00:47.760
when winter cold gives
way to springtime light.

00:00:47.770 --> 00:00:51.790
The 1987 Montreal Protocol
phased out the chemicals.

00:00:51.790 --> 00:00:55.000
And measurements have shown them 
declining in the atmosphere.

00:00:55.010 --> 00:00:59.090
Now, researchers have found evidence
of the intended consequence:

00:00:59.090 --> 00:01:01.760
shrinkage in the ozone hole.

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:03.280
Using satellites,

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:04.920
ground-based instruments,

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:07.280
and ozone-measuring weather balloons,

00:01:07.280 --> 00:01:11.260
they showed that since 2000, the
Antarctic hole in September

00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:14.400
shrunk by 4 million square kilometers --

00:01:14.400 --> 00:01:16.660
an area bigger than India.

00:01:16.660 --> 00:01:19.900
An extra challenge was
showing that the shrinkage

00:01:19.900 --> 00:01:22.640
was due to the drop in chemicals.

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:26.080
Using a 3D atmospheric model,
the researchers

00:01:26.080 --> 00:01:30.280
separated out the effects of
weather and volcanic emissions,

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:32.700
which can also destroy ozone.

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:35.040
The model helped explain anomalously large

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:37.960
ozone holes like the one in October 2015 -

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:40.880
which was largely due to the
eruption earlier that year

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:44.140
of the Calbuco volcano in southern Chile.

00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:46.320
Although the hole has begun to heal,

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:48.320
the chlorine and bromine
containing chemicals

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:50.500
have long atmospheric lifetimes.

00:01:50.500 --> 00:01:52.200
So scientists don’t expect the hole

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:56.220
to close up completely until
2050, at the earliest.

